course: dictionary
lesson: 3315
title: The euro
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The euro is money. It is used in many countries of the European Union, such as Ireland, France, Germany, Spain and Italy. Its sign is €, and one euro is 100 cents.
explain: Look at the words around the word euro to understand it. «In the UK coins go from 1p to £2; Australia, Canada and New Zealand use cents and dollar coins, and Ireland uses euro cents and euro coins.» This sentence compares kinds of money, so euro must be a kind of money, like pence or dollars.
explain: Small words give clues too. «Five euros» means five units of money, and «€2» means two euros. A euro is one unit, so when a number comes before it, count that many euros. Check your meaning by swapping it in: «Ireland uses euro cents» still makes sense if you think of euro as money.
fact: The euro is the money used in many countries of the European Union, including Ireland, France, Germany, Spain and Italy.
fact: The sign for the euro is €.
fact: One euro is 100 cents, so «euros and cents» shows an amount of money.
fact: «Euro» is a noun: you can say «a euro coin» or «five euros».
fact: In France a price may be written as «9,50 €», which means nine euros fifty.
exercise: What is the sign for the euro? | ¥ | $ | £ | *€
exercise: If one euro is 100 cents, how many cents are in two euros? | 2 cents | 20 cents | *200 cents | 100 cents
exercise: Which price means nine euros fifty? | 9,05 € | 95 € | 950 € | *9,50 €
test: Which country uses the euro? | New Zealand | Canada | Australia | *Italy
test: What does «€2» mean? | *Two euros | Two cents | Two pounds | Twenty euros
exam: Maya has «five euros» and spends three euros. How much money is left? | *2 euros | 2 cents | 3 euros | 8 euros
